00:08:19.489 --> 00:08:20.986
you can have input for the cover,

00:08:20.986 --> 00:08:23.289
but they, I was in charge
of the words inside.

00:08:23.289 --> 00:08:24.122
- It looked like you had ear wax

00:08:24.122 --> 00:08:25.420
with a plane coming out.
- I know, and I've heard,

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:27.617
in fact if the see the,
I've heard other comments

00:08:27.617 --> 00:08:30.218
which we can't mention
about what that looks like,

00:08:30.218 --> 00:08:31.551
so yes, I agree.

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:35.860
- So the two of us saw a
prescreening of, "First Man",

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:40.070
and everything I know
about Neil Armstrong,

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:42.201
'cause I knew him, I mean,

00:08:42.201 --> 00:08:43.310
I don't claim I knew him well,

00:08:43.310 --> 00:08:44.607
we weren't beer-drinking buddies,

00:08:44.607 --> 00:08:47.943
but, I mean, we were
acquaintances I should say.

00:08:47.943 --> 00:08:50.277
And everything I knew about him,

00:08:50.277 --> 00:08:51.925
and I think is true for you,

00:08:51.925 --> 00:08:55.783
all that you about him,
all that you knew of him,

00:08:55.783 --> 00:08:57.594
was consistent with how he
was portrayed in this film,

00:08:57.594 --> 00:08:58.890
would you agree?

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:00.752
- Absolutely, yeah, everything
that I knew about him.

00:09:00.752 --> 00:09:01.687
(voice covers voice)
- So give me

00:09:01.687 --> 00:09:02.857
your best characterization of him

00:09:02.857 --> 00:09:04.477
'cause some people don't even know that.

00:09:04.477 --> 00:09:06.810
- I would say that he loved,

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:11.769
he loved flying airplanes,
he loved doing his job,

00:09:11.769 --> 00:09:13.113
he loved being a test pilot.
- He was fighter,

00:09:13.113 --> 00:09:13.991
test pilot, he was

00:09:13.991 --> 00:09:15.204
a fighter pilot.
- A fighter pilot first.

00:09:15.204 --> 00:09:16.413
- In Korea.
- Yep.

00:09:16.413 --> 00:09:17.877
- Then test pilot.
- Then a test pilot.

00:09:17.877 --> 00:09:21.544
He was, I guess, a very
thoughtful engineer,

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:23.923
but loved flying.

00:09:23.923 --> 00:09:25.193
When he came and spoke to our

00:09:25.193 --> 00:09:27.860
astronaut class--
- Oh, at that engineer club.

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:31.164
- Well he was, but he saw it
as an engineering problem,

00:09:31.164 --> 00:09:32.724
as a challenge, and that's why I think

00:09:32.724 --> 00:09:35.024
he was not just a great
pilot 'cause he loved flying,

00:09:35.024 --> 00:09:37.707
but also a great test
pilot because he enjoyed

00:09:37.707 --> 00:09:39.586
the engineering behind it.

00:09:39.586 --> 00:09:41.588
And that's, that was pretty impressive

00:09:41.588 --> 00:09:42.421
I thought.
- That's a,

00:09:42.421 --> 00:09:43.254
I had never thought

00:09:43.254 --> 00:09:44.087
about that.
- Yeah.

00:09:44.087 --> 00:09:45.564
- You could be fly boy and
say, give me that machine

00:09:45.564 --> 00:09:47.208
I'll do it what I get,
but if you're an engineer

00:09:47.208 --> 00:09:48.041
you're thinking about

00:09:48.041 --> 00:09:48.874
that machine.
- Right.

00:09:48.874 --> 00:09:49.709
- And if the aerodynamics,

00:09:49.709 --> 00:09:51.689
the everything.
- If you're really into that,

00:09:51.689 --> 00:09:54.755
like he was, I think it was this,

00:09:54.755 --> 00:09:56.705
and not all great engineers I think

00:09:56.705 --> 00:09:59.331
can make great pilots, but he
was one of those that could

00:09:59.331 --> 00:10:00.164
and I think that's where you have

00:10:00.164 --> 00:10:01.412
a really special test pilot.

00:10:01.412 --> 00:10:04.646
- And do they ever make a
change to the plane and, or,

00:10:04.646 --> 00:10:08.542
and he says, no, that ain't gonna work?

00:10:08.542 --> 00:10:09.685
- I'm sure he chimed in.

00:10:09.685 --> 00:10:12.601
I would expect that those
conversations were made,

00:10:12.601 --> 00:10:15.351
especially back in those days
when they were doing things

00:10:15.351 --> 00:10:17.861
that were much different

00:10:17.861 --> 00:10:19.784
than what they'd ever done before

00:10:19.784 --> 00:10:21.234
and how fast they were going,
how high they were going,

00:10:21.234 --> 00:10:23.027
and what they were trying to achieve.

00:10:23.027 --> 00:10:26.005
When his test pilot days there was,

00:10:26.005 --> 00:10:28.043
I'm sure there was a lot of
those conversations, yeah.

00:10:28.043 --> 00:10:31.279
- So, I would add to
that that Neil Armstrong

00:10:31.279 --> 00:10:32.862
was not gregarious,

00:10:34.685 --> 00:10:35.518
he was a very--
- That's right.

00:10:35.518 --> 00:10:38.297
- Quiet man, did not seek publicity,

00:10:38.297 --> 00:10:40.511
did not, you know, he's not the person

00:10:40.511 --> 00:10:41.344
you'd say is the life

00:10:41.344 --> 00:10:42.372
of the party.
- Yep.

00:10:42.372 --> 00:10:43.352
- But sometimes the people

00:10:43.352 --> 00:10:44.626
who are not the life of the party

00:10:44.626 --> 00:10:46.112
are sitting there doing nothing

00:10:46.112 --> 00:10:48.762
he's sitting there in his
head figuring stuff out,

00:10:48.762 --> 00:10:51.809
it's the active restless
brain of the engineer.

00:10:51.809 --> 00:10:53.613
And so this would

00:10:53.613 --> 00:10:54.953
surely capture--
- That was him

00:10:54.953 --> 00:10:56.235
and when I first met him, Neil,

00:10:56.235 --> 00:10:58.344
you described that really well.

00:10:58.344 --> 00:11:00.153
When he got up there in front of our,

00:11:00.153 --> 00:11:02.233
we all stood up and gave
him a standing ovation

00:11:02.233 --> 00:11:04.752
and just about all, I was
one of the younger people

00:11:04.752 --> 00:11:07.381
in that group of new astronauts in '96,

00:11:07.381 --> 00:11:09.679
so just about everyone in that room,

00:11:09.679 --> 00:11:11.461
maybe one or two wouldn't remember,

00:11:11.461 --> 00:11:15.665
remembered where they were,
and he in that episode

00:11:15.665 --> 00:11:19.460
of what he did, landing on
the Moon, that whole mission,

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:21.861
inspired most of us to become
astronauts I would say,

00:11:21.861 --> 00:11:23.399
we all remembered it.

00:11:23.399 --> 00:11:24.831
And--
- So you're meeting your hero?

00:11:24.831 --> 00:11:26.220
- We're meeting our hero,
and it wasn't just me,

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:27.053
it was everybody.

00:11:27.053 --> 00:11:30.467
And he was, he's the man,
right, he was the man.

00:11:30.467 --> 00:11:31.689
And he gets up there and was,

00:11:31.689 --> 00:11:34.439
it seemed almost like
he was painfully shy,

00:11:34.439 --> 00:11:36.717
almost that it was hard for him to talk.

00:11:36.717 --> 00:11:39.697
And he didn't mention the Moon at all,

00:11:39.697 --> 00:11:42.820
he talked about test flying
and how important that is

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:44.781
and how you have to be diligent about it

00:11:44.781 --> 00:11:47.145
and how much he loved doing that.

00:11:47.145 --> 00:11:49.026
And after he was done when we
got to questions and answers

00:11:49.026 --> 00:11:50.930
then we started asking him,
what was it like on the Moon?

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:53.689
But up to that point he was
delivering that message,

00:11:53.689 --> 00:11:57.027
almost painfully shy, but he was...

00:11:57.027 --> 00:11:58.787
He loved so much what he did

00:11:58.787 --> 00:12:00.206
and felt it was so important

00:12:00.206 --> 00:12:01.447
that that's what he focused on.

00:12:01.447 --> 00:12:03.395
He was the right man for the job.

00:12:03.395 --> 00:12:08.145
- Do you think NASA chose him
to be the first on the Moon

00:12:10.993 --> 00:12:12.459
because of all this?

00:12:12.459 --> 00:12:14.284
Because he does not seek publicity,

00:12:14.284 --> 00:12:15.363
because, 'cause if they got

00:12:15.363 --> 00:12:17.178
some grandstanding--
- Yeah.

00:12:17.178 --> 00:12:18.431
- Y'all look at me

00:12:18.431 --> 00:12:20.202
I'm on the Moon.
- Yep, yep.

00:12:20.202 --> 00:12:22.151
- Then here's my book
about me being on the Moon

00:12:22.151 --> 00:12:24.184
and here's my talk show, interview.

00:12:24.184 --> 00:12:27.463
- I've got a need for speed.
- Right.

00:12:27.463 --> 00:12:28.296
- You mean if it was like one of us?

00:12:28.296 --> 00:12:29.129
(laughing)

00:12:29.129 --> 00:12:29.962
Is that what you're

00:12:29.962 --> 00:12:30.795
basically saying?
- No.

00:12:30.795 --> 00:12:33.375
- Yeah, no that's--
- Do you think

00:12:33.375 --> 00:12:35.236
they thought that through?

00:12:35.236 --> 00:12:38.009
- I think that what they--
- They picked someone humble.

00:12:38.009 --> 00:12:40.781
- You know that seems like a,

00:12:40.781 --> 00:12:43.507
I used to think that maybe at first,

00:12:43.507 --> 00:12:45.224
but I think lately in the last few years

00:12:45.224 --> 00:12:47.744
I changed my thinking of it

00:12:47.744 --> 00:12:49.968
because I think that's
almost too much thinking.

00:12:49.968 --> 00:12:51.427
I think really what they wanted to do,

00:12:51.427 --> 00:12:53.233
no really, I think what
they were looking for

00:12:53.233 --> 00:12:54.300
was a guy--
- I think that was

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:55.521
too much thinking.
- No, 'cause you're,

00:12:55.521 --> 00:12:57.390
I guess you start--
- You're overthinking.

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:58.916
- You start thinking too many things

00:12:58.916 --> 00:13:01.216
about this guy is wearing
blue and this you, you know,

00:13:01.216 --> 00:13:02.049
you overthink it.

00:13:02.049 --> 00:13:05.429
I think what they saw was
this was the right man

00:13:05.429 --> 00:13:07.352
to land on the Moon.

00:13:07.352 --> 00:13:08.685
Whether or not he was gregarious,

00:13:08.685 --> 00:13:10.422
whether or not he was shy,

00:13:10.422 --> 00:13:12.017
whether or not, whatever those personality

00:13:12.017 --> 00:13:13.094
traits were--
- Had ice in his veins.

00:13:13.094 --> 00:13:14.469
- He was the right man

00:13:14.469 --> 00:13:17.022
because he understood what was happening,

00:13:17.022 --> 00:13:20.214
he was gonna focus on that job 100%,

00:13:20.214 --> 00:13:23.316
not be distracted, and
maybe that has partly to do

00:13:23.316 --> 00:13:25.309
with the fame-seeking, but I think really

00:13:25.309 --> 00:13:29.060
he was chosen not for that,
for the personality part of it,

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:31.393
but because he was the right man to

00:13:31.393 --> 00:13:32.285
do that job.
- So, did they

00:13:32.285 --> 00:13:34.944
chose who actually got out of the

00:13:34.944 --> 00:13:36.380
capsule first.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:37.213
- I mean--
- Yeah, that's

00:13:37.213 --> 00:13:38.046
the whole thing.
- And so,

00:13:38.046 --> 00:13:38.879
but he was the mission

00:13:38.879 --> 00:13:40.192
lead as well.
- The mission commander.

00:13:40.192 --> 00:13:41.148
- Yes.
- He was the commander,

00:13:41.148 --> 00:13:42.380
that's right.
- And so,

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:45.756
it wasn't because he was
commander that he got to go first,

00:13:45.756 --> 00:13:47.131
they actually made the choice like,

00:13:47.131 --> 00:13:50.191
you're gonna be the first
to step foot on the Moon.

00:13:50.191 --> 00:13:51.797
- And you're commander.
- And you're commander.

00:13:51.797 --> 00:13:53.284
Like those are two separate things?

00:13:53.284 --> 00:13:54.417
- You think.
- Yeah, and like,

00:13:54.417 --> 00:13:55.526
for example--
- It could have been

00:13:55.526 --> 00:13:56.439
like in Star Trek--
- Yep.

00:13:56.439 --> 00:13:57.827
- You go check out the glowing

00:13:57.827 --> 00:13:59.179
blob first.
- Right.

00:13:59.179 --> 00:14:00.927
- (laughing) Buzz, you check,

00:14:00.927 --> 00:14:03.283
see that glowing thing, report

00:14:03.283 --> 00:14:04.747
back to me--
- Right.

00:14:04.747 --> 00:14:05.639
- And then I'll step off the,

00:14:05.639 --> 00:14:06.974
off the--
- And that's traditionally...

00:14:06.974 --> 00:14:09.362
- You're gonna be the black
ensign from the Enterprise.

00:14:09.362 --> 00:14:11.738
- But that's the way,
that's the way we did it,

00:14:11.738 --> 00:14:14.619
now that your saying it that's
why I space walked apparently

00:14:14.619 --> 00:14:16.730
because that's what we would
do in the shuttle program,

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:18.347
the commander and the pilot

00:14:18.347 --> 00:14:19.798
would not go out and space walk,

00:14:19.798 --> 00:14:21.030
the mission specialists would.

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:22.016
And the underlying, one of the

00:14:22.016 --> 00:14:22.849
underlying reasons was--
- Just because

00:14:22.849 --> 00:14:26.038
a mission specialists is
someone who has an expertise,

00:14:26.038 --> 00:14:28.437
usually a scientific or
an engineering expertise,

00:14:28.437 --> 00:14:29.270
brought into the service

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:30.526
of the mission.
- Correct.

00:14:30.526 --> 00:14:31.359
- So you're not flying

00:14:31.359 --> 00:14:32.305
the plane--
We're not there

00:14:32.305 --> 00:14:33.856
for the landing.
- Right.

00:14:33.856 --> 00:14:35.805
- We're not gonna land, I
mean we were part of landing,

00:14:35.805 --> 00:14:36.638
but we're not actually

00:14:36.638 --> 00:14:37.471
gonna land.
- Right.

00:14:37.471 --> 00:14:38.840
- 'Cause the idea is what happens

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:40.385
if your commander goes
out and doesn't come back

00:14:40.385 --> 00:14:41.684
who'd gonna land?
- Right.

00:14:41.684 --> 00:14:44.250
But it's okay if you go
out and don't come back.

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:45.367
You could still land the--
- I hate to put it that way,

00:14:45.367 --> 00:14:47.093
- Land the bird.
- But yes.

00:14:47.093 --> 00:14:48.689
(voices cover voices)

00:14:48.689 --> 00:14:50.130
When we used to, we used to
brief for our space walks

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:52.247
there was a lost crewman,
there was a line,

00:14:52.247 --> 00:14:54.063
everything you would check like all right,

00:14:54.063 --> 00:14:56.579
this is in place, this check,
that check, that check,

00:14:56.579 --> 00:14:58.770
and part of the briefing
was, lost crewman.

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:00.707
And lost crewman was a procedure we had

00:15:00.707 --> 00:15:02.442
to go rescue a guy that becomes lost.

00:15:02.442 --> 00:15:04.391
And what we would do,
sort of kidding around,

00:15:04.391 --> 00:15:06.495
was lost crewman, don't worry about it,

00:15:06.495 --> 00:15:07.328
we got three more.

00:15:07.328 --> 00:15:08.161
(laughing)

00:15:08.161 --> 00:15:09.567
That's what we, because
we had four space walkers,

00:15:09.567 --> 00:15:11.591
so that was like the
joke and we'd all laugh,

00:15:11.591 --> 00:15:14.463
but underlying like, you'll come get me,

00:15:14.463 --> 00:15:15.631
but we were gonna do that if we needed.

00:15:15.631 --> 00:15:17.207
(laughing)

00:15:17.207 --> 00:15:18.439
- What were we talking about?

00:15:18.439 --> 00:15:19.319
Oh, the commander, who went

00:15:19.319 --> 00:15:20.152
out first.
- Yeah, the commander.

00:15:20.152 --> 00:15:22.980
- And traditionally, I think
in Gemini what they did was,

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:24.017
is that the commander would never

00:15:24.017 --> 00:15:24.850
go outside--
- Gemini,

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:26.185
two astronaut capsule.
- Right,

00:15:26.185 --> 00:15:27.242
and that's when they first space walked,

00:15:27.242 --> 00:15:29.648
Ed White was the first space walker,

00:15:29.648 --> 00:15:33.231
and Buzz was one of the last
space walkers in Gemini,

00:15:33.231 --> 00:15:34.064
but I think it

00:15:34.064 --> 00:15:34.897
was tradition--
- Buzz Aldrin.

00:15:34.897 --> 00:15:36.485
- The commander stayed
inside, yeah, thank you,

00:15:36.485 --> 00:15:38.434
but mainly a command, I
think the tradition was

00:15:38.434 --> 00:15:40.050
the commander stayed inside

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:41.985
and it was the pilot who went out

00:15:41.985 --> 00:15:42.818
and then came back,

00:15:42.818 --> 00:15:44.441
'cause only one guy at a time going.

00:15:44.441 --> 00:15:45.762
So this was a different case

00:15:45.762 --> 00:15:47.586
where you're gonna have
both people going out

00:15:47.586 --> 00:15:49.169
for the walk.
- Wow.

00:15:50.138 --> 00:15:50.971
- Yeah.

00:15:50.971 --> 00:15:53.130
- Part of the authenticity of the film

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:55.032
was there are little details

00:15:55.032 --> 00:15:57.013
that they didn't have
to really care about,

00:15:57.013 --> 00:15:58.410
but they did.
- Yes.

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:02.150
- So there's a moment, I
happen to own an Omega watch

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:03.318
that was gifted to me

00:16:03.318 --> 00:16:05.101
by Stephen Hawking.
- Wow.

00:16:05.101 --> 00:16:07.279
- And.
- That's pretty nice.

00:16:07.279 --> 00:16:08.112
- Yeah.
- I don't mean

00:16:08.112 --> 00:16:08.945
to name drop.
- I was gonna say

00:16:08.945 --> 00:16:11.946
I just like the fact that
you didn't name drop.

00:16:11.946 --> 00:16:14.016
- I've got one too, but
I had to buy it myself.

00:16:14.016 --> 00:16:14.949
- Right.
- No, no.

00:16:14.949 --> 00:16:16.504
- I have a Stephen Hawking watch too,

00:16:16.504 --> 00:16:17.869
he just doesn't know I have it.

00:16:17.869 --> 00:16:19.025
(laughing)

00:16:19.025 --> 00:16:20.070
- We got his watch.
- Yeah, he was

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:20.982
just walking around just like,

00:16:20.982 --> 00:16:23.292
anybody seen my watch?

00:16:23.292 --> 00:16:24.316
Who has seen my watch?

00:16:24.316 --> 00:16:26.744
(laughing)

00:16:26.744 --> 00:16:29.126
- So (laughing).

00:16:29.126 --> 00:16:30.654
- It was gifted to you by you.

00:16:30.654 --> 00:16:32.040
- Exactly.

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:34.813
- So, no I got the Stephen Hawking Award

00:16:34.813 --> 00:16:37.200
for Science Communication,

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:38.949
so it's only like a year old,

00:16:38.949 --> 00:16:40.456
but this--
- Cool man.

00:16:40.456 --> 00:16:41.289
- This introduced me to

00:16:41.289 --> 00:16:42.528
Omega watches.
- Yeah.

00:16:42.528 --> 00:16:43.868
- Omega was the first watch

00:16:43.868 --> 00:16:45.036
on the Moon.
- Right.

00:16:45.036 --> 00:16:49.160
- They were chosen by NASA
after NASA got all the premier,

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:51.624
what the Rolex, Breitling,

00:16:51.624 --> 00:16:54.472
whatever the top watches were of the day,

00:16:54.472 --> 00:16:55.915
I wonder if they threw in a Timex,

00:16:55.915 --> 00:16:57.748
I don't know, just to get America

00:16:57.748 --> 00:16:59.159
in there.
- I bet so.

00:16:59.159 --> 00:17:00.711
- I'm sure they did.
- I'm sure they did.

00:17:00.711 --> 00:17:01.788
- Okay, so they throw it in.
