00:25:57.962 --> 00:25:59.350
it was brief, but I have it

00:25:59.350 --> 00:26:02.095
and you'll see that he's not a,

00:26:02.095 --> 00:26:04.762
he's smart and calm and measured

00:26:06.086 --> 00:26:07.680
and let's check it out.
- Cool.

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:09.208
- This is my interview
with Neil Armstrong,

00:26:09.208 --> 00:26:10.925
brief though it was.

00:26:10.925 --> 00:26:14.829
Neil Armstrong, commander of Apollo 11.

00:26:14.829 --> 00:26:18.305
How old were you 40 years ago today?

00:26:18.305 --> 00:26:20.305
- [Neil A.] I was 38.93.

00:26:22.705 --> 00:26:25.049
- [Neil] Excellent, I love it.

00:26:25.049 --> 00:26:27.261
And of the entire Apollo era

00:26:27.261 --> 00:26:29.461
what's your most indelible memory?

00:26:29.461 --> 00:26:31.397
It could be your own walk, but if not,

00:26:31.397 --> 00:26:32.255
I'd just be curious.

00:26:32.255 --> 00:26:33.458
- [Neil A.] The most indelible memory

00:26:33.458 --> 00:26:38.458
was approaching the Moon and
flying through the Moon shadow

00:26:39.178 --> 00:26:41.166
so that the Moon was eclipsing the sun

00:26:41.166 --> 00:26:44.708
and we could see the
corona all around the Moon.

00:26:44.708 --> 00:26:47.052
It was not circular, it was elliptical,

00:26:47.052 --> 00:26:49.628
which was a big surprise,
I didn't understand that.

00:26:49.628 --> 00:26:51.431
And then we could see the Moon,

00:26:51.431 --> 00:26:53.586
the dark side of the Moon, of course,

00:26:53.586 --> 00:26:55.835
illuminated by Earth light

00:26:55.835 --> 00:26:59.024
and we could see the
craters and the valleys

00:26:59.024 --> 00:27:03.357
and the plains in a blue-gray
three-dimensional view

00:27:05.783 --> 00:27:06.616
that was spectacular.

00:27:06.616 --> 00:27:10.703
- The texture, the image.
- And remarkable,

00:27:10.703 --> 00:27:13.203
but imperceptible to a camera,

00:27:14.154 --> 00:27:16.310
but the human eye was wonderful.

00:27:16.310 --> 00:27:18.043
- [Neil] And the last question,

00:27:18.043 --> 00:27:21.184
what do you think NASA should do next?

00:27:21.184 --> 00:27:23.790
- [Neil A.] I'm supportive
of the NASA plan

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:26.459
and I'm--
- Just needs more money

00:27:26.459 --> 00:27:28.758
I suppose, but the ideas are there.

00:27:28.758 --> 00:27:32.386
- [Neil A.] Yeah, I think
the approach they're on

00:27:32.386 --> 00:27:35.303
is a good one.
- I like that

00:27:35.303 --> 00:27:37.779
that's a very pilot, the approach here.

00:27:37.779 --> 00:27:40.838
(laughing)

00:27:40.838 --> 00:27:42.600
- [Neil A.] They're a
little below (mumbles)

00:27:42.600 --> 00:27:45.043
but, you know, they're gonna get there.

00:27:45.043 --> 00:27:45.876
- [Neil] All right, Neil Armstrong,

00:27:45.876 --> 00:27:47.451
thanks for those three questions.

00:27:47.451 --> 00:27:48.284
- Wow.
- Yeah.

00:27:48.284 --> 00:27:50.303
- That's pretty cool.
- The guy's never interviewed

00:27:50.303 --> 00:27:52.723
and I felt like I was even taking too much

00:27:52.723 --> 00:27:53.768
by asking just those

00:27:53.768 --> 00:27:55.230
three questions.
- Yeah.

00:27:55.230 --> 00:27:59.202
- And, see I'm kinda giddy--
- He was really into

00:27:59.202 --> 00:28:01.845
that second, what's the indelible,

00:28:01.845 --> 00:28:03.253
you could actually--
- Yeah.

00:28:03.253 --> 00:28:05.545
- I can almost feel him--
- Looking and experiencing it.

00:28:05.545 --> 00:28:07.779
- Looking at the Moon, yes--
- And experiencing it.

00:28:07.779 --> 00:28:09.966
- Like it was really very visceral,

00:28:09.966 --> 00:28:11.662
he's recounting.
- And consider

00:28:11.662 --> 00:28:13.831
that's nothing you're gonna
get on this side of the Moon.

00:28:13.831 --> 00:28:14.711
- Yeah.
- Right.

00:28:14.711 --> 00:28:16.010
- So it had to be the back side

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:17.023
'cause he studied, he would've studied

00:28:17.023 --> 00:28:19.718
all the maps and pictures and everything.

00:28:19.718 --> 00:28:20.590
- Right.
- Yeah.

00:28:20.590 --> 00:28:23.327
- And you know one of the things that,

00:28:23.327 --> 00:28:24.342
that I noticed with the movie,

00:28:24.342 --> 00:28:25.189
that I liked,

00:28:25.189 --> 00:28:26.323
one of my favorites--
- The "First Man".

00:28:26.323 --> 00:28:28.784
- The "First Man" movie,

00:28:28.784 --> 00:28:30.967
was that you're able to
see what it looked like

00:28:30.967 --> 00:28:33.454
and I think they probably
did it pretty accurately.

00:28:33.454 --> 00:28:36.367
Because the film that we
had back then, in 1969,

00:28:36.367 --> 00:28:39.338
we had some, but especially of
the approach and the landing,

00:28:39.338 --> 00:28:40.849
if you remember, like the
camera is kinda looking out

00:28:40.849 --> 00:28:43.334
that triangular window
and the dust kicks up

00:28:43.334 --> 00:28:45.345
and you really don't get an appreciation

00:28:45.345 --> 00:28:47.538
for what it was like to see outside.

00:28:47.538 --> 00:28:49.430
Can you imagine now if
we were able to do that

00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:51.695
with a bunch of GoPros, or
whatever they would stick on,

00:28:51.695 --> 00:28:53.049
you know high def cameras,

00:28:53.049 --> 00:28:54.469
we would see that moonscape.

00:28:54.469 --> 00:28:56.217
And probably even the night passes

00:28:56.217 --> 00:28:57.618
(voice covers voice) describe.
- A GoPro every foot.

00:28:57.618 --> 00:28:59.432
- Probably so--
- Mounted on the thing.

00:28:59.432 --> 00:29:00.265
- And right, you know.

00:29:00.265 --> 00:29:01.713
- Let's make the whole--
- And it's easy right?

00:29:01.713 --> 00:29:03.144
- Make the whole ship out of GoPros.

00:29:03.144 --> 00:29:04.577
- (laughing) That's--
- And now,

00:29:04.577 --> 00:29:06.557
and even in the low-light
level on the other side

00:29:06.557 --> 00:29:07.666
I'm sure they could've found something,

00:29:07.666 --> 00:29:09.175
you know they would've
been able to do something.

00:29:09.175 --> 00:29:11.553
'Cause just recently now
we can get great images

00:29:11.553 --> 00:29:14.440
of the planet at night from
the station, for example.

00:29:14.440 --> 00:29:17.111
- And if it wasn't clear his
point that the eye catches it,

00:29:17.111 --> 00:29:18.257
but the camera doesn't--
- Yeah.

00:29:18.257 --> 00:29:19.961
- Is 'cause the eye in one glimpse

00:29:19.961 --> 00:29:22.557
can get a very high-dynamic range.

00:29:22.557 --> 00:29:23.724
So the Moon can be very dim,

00:29:23.724 --> 00:29:25.703
but the solar corona could be very bright

00:29:25.703 --> 00:29:27.067
and you can see all that at once.

00:29:27.067 --> 00:29:29.048
With the camera it's gonna commit

00:29:29.048 --> 00:29:31.153
to either the bright
corona, or the dim thing,

00:29:31.153 --> 00:29:32.451
but you're not gonna get both.
- Right.

00:29:32.451 --> 00:29:34.179
- And he's experiencing both.

00:29:34.179 --> 00:29:36.183
- That description he gave you

00:29:36.183 --> 00:29:39.019
allowed us to picture what it was like.

00:29:39.019 --> 00:29:42.035
There's no real good video of that,

00:29:42.035 --> 00:29:43.847
but his description of it is what we

00:29:43.847 --> 00:29:44.939
have to go on.
- Yeah, that's so cool,

00:29:44.939 --> 00:29:46.643
like yeah.
- But you can feel that

00:29:46.643 --> 00:29:50.883
he'd rather just not be,
be interviewed, right,

00:29:50.883 --> 00:29:51.817
it's he just wanna

00:29:51.817 --> 00:29:53.589
go on his way.
- Yeah.

00:29:53.589 --> 00:29:55.108
- But is that really the best person

00:29:55.108 --> 00:29:57.384
to have represent the fact

00:29:57.384 --> 00:30:01.564
that you have walked on the
surface of a celestial body?

00:30:01.564 --> 00:30:03.319
- That's a good question.
- You know.

00:30:03.319 --> 00:30:04.572
I've heard one of his,
I heard Mike Collins

00:30:04.572 --> 00:30:06.247
at Neil's memorial--
- Mike Collins

00:30:06.247 --> 00:30:07.877
the third astronaut who didn't get to go

00:30:07.877 --> 00:30:08.931
down to the Moon.
- Right he was,

00:30:08.931 --> 00:30:13.786
yeah he orbited in lunar
orbit in the Command Module

00:30:13.786 --> 00:30:16.241
while Neil and, Neil
Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin

00:30:16.241 --> 00:30:17.658
were on the Moon.

00:30:18.858 --> 00:30:21.181
I heard him speak at the memorial,

00:30:21.181 --> 00:30:22.237
I don't wanna misquote him,

00:30:22.237 --> 00:30:24.789
but at Neil Armstrong's memorial,

00:30:24.789 --> 00:30:27.218
when he died and they had
a memorial service for him

00:30:27.218 --> 00:30:29.066
at the Johnson Space Center,

00:30:29.066 --> 00:30:30.465
and he talked a little bit about that

00:30:30.465 --> 00:30:34.149
about him being maybe shy and cerebral,

00:30:34.149 --> 00:30:36.031
or whatever, but he was
like, well who would you,

00:30:36.031 --> 00:30:37.415
why wouldn't you want that?

00:30:37.415 --> 00:30:39.949
Why wouldn't you want this
man who was so qualified,

00:30:39.949 --> 00:30:41.742
who did such a great job,

00:30:41.742 --> 00:30:42.863
why you want him to be anything different

00:30:42.863 --> 00:30:44.546
than who he was?

00:30:44.546 --> 00:30:46.262
And I think that's, and
I think that that's,

00:30:46.262 --> 00:30:48.210
he was the right man to land on the Moon

00:30:48.210 --> 00:30:51.278
and I think that was what
they were most concerned with.

00:30:51.278 --> 00:30:53.962
Because, no kidding, they weren't so sure

00:30:53.962 --> 00:30:55.810
they were coming back from that mission.

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:58.550
They weren't so sure they
were gonna be successful.

00:30:58.550 --> 00:31:02.001
Apollo 12 and 13, 11, 12 and
13, all had the same mission.

00:31:02.001 --> 00:31:03.665
They all trained for the same mission

00:31:03.665 --> 00:31:06.781
because they weren't so sure
11 was gonna be successful

00:31:06.781 --> 00:31:07.614
and then it was and so then 12

00:31:07.614 --> 00:31:08.693
had to come up with something quick,

00:31:08.693 --> 00:31:11.257
which was different than
what they did on 11.

00:31:11.257 --> 00:31:12.973
But they all trained for that same mission

00:31:12.973 --> 00:31:15.406
'cause they weren't so
sure 11 was gonna be,

00:31:15.406 --> 00:31:16.497
was gonna be successful.
- What you're saying

00:31:16.497 --> 00:31:18.929
is 11 failed then--
- 12 would try.

00:31:18.929 --> 00:31:19.995
- Well next up--
- You try it, right.

00:31:19.995 --> 00:31:21.449
- You're next.
- Wow.

00:31:21.449 --> 00:31:24.113
- If that failed, you're next.
- 13 was gonna try it

00:31:24.113 --> 00:31:25.852
and that can be a few different ways.

00:31:25.852 --> 00:31:28.393
Not so that would get, that
they wouldn't come back alive,

00:31:28.393 --> 00:31:30.593
but they might not get down
to the surface and come back,

00:31:30.593 --> 00:31:32.837
they would have to abort
and then come back to Earth.

00:31:32.837 --> 00:31:34.750
So it was really important for them

00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:38.006
to try to get the right
guy to be the first guy.

00:31:38.006 --> 00:31:38.839
- And they--
- And that's what

00:31:38.839 --> 00:31:40.141
they went with, who's the best guy

00:31:40.141 --> 00:31:42.978
to pull off the landing especially of this

00:31:42.978 --> 00:31:43.811
and then come back alive.
- And that's why

00:31:43.811 --> 00:31:44.941
he had ice in his veins.

00:31:44.941 --> 00:31:47.966
And by the way, there's
a misconception, I think,

00:31:47.966 --> 00:31:51.049
about the first comments from Houston

00:31:52.433 --> 00:31:55.400
after he says, Houston,
Tranquility base here,

00:31:55.400 --> 00:31:57.018
Eagle has landed.
- Yep.

00:31:57.018 --> 00:32:00.020
- Okay, which means, of
course the first word,

00:32:00.020 --> 00:32:02.475
or the first comments
from the Moon is, Houston.

00:32:02.475 --> 00:32:04.662
- There you go.
(laughing)

00:32:04.662 --> 00:32:07.438
- A plug for Houston,
Texas, my former home.

00:32:07.438 --> 00:32:08.868
- The planet, Houston.
- Yep.

00:32:08.868 --> 00:32:10.695
- So, Houston, Tranquility

00:32:10.695 --> 00:32:11.953
base here.
- That's the way we talk.

00:32:11.953 --> 00:32:13.084
- Actually there was some other,

00:32:13.084 --> 00:32:14.041
contact light and

00:32:14.041 --> 00:32:14.874
other things.
- Yeah, contact light.

00:32:14.874 --> 00:32:18.500
- Right, but Houston
then says something like,

00:32:18.500 --> 00:32:21.415
congratulations guys, you
have a bunch of guys down here

00:32:21.415 --> 00:32:23.433
who were about to turn blue.
- Yeah,

00:32:23.433 --> 00:32:24.393
that was Charlie Duke.

00:32:24.393 --> 00:32:25.482
- Okay.
- Yep.

00:32:25.482 --> 00:32:26.682
- You think they're saying that

00:32:26.682 --> 00:32:28.451
because they just landed on the Moon,

00:32:28.451 --> 00:32:30.642
that's not why they're saying it.

00:32:30.642 --> 00:32:32.245
- They were holding their breath?

00:32:32.245 --> 00:32:33.995
- That's not, yes, they
were holding their breath,

00:32:33.995 --> 00:32:36.534
but it's not because they
just landed on the Moon,

00:32:36.534 --> 00:32:37.998
that's not why.
- Okay why were,

00:32:37.998 --> 00:32:39.769
okay, so why were they about to turn?

00:32:39.769 --> 00:32:42.070
Wait, they were Smurfs,
no I'm only kidding.

00:32:42.070 --> 00:32:43.458
(laughing)

00:32:43.458 --> 00:32:45.524
I'm sorry I had to do that.
- Because Neil

00:32:45.524 --> 00:32:48.758
was not happy with the
original landing spot

00:32:48.758 --> 00:32:51.070
and he only has a certain amount of fuel

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:53.710
to prevent himself from
crashing down onto the Moon,

00:32:53.710 --> 00:32:55.171
this is keeping them buoyant.
- Yep.

00:32:55.171 --> 00:32:56.227
- Nope, too many boulders there,

00:32:56.227 --> 00:32:57.909
nope, too many boulders there,

00:32:57.909 --> 00:33:01.134
and you see the fuel
come down, wait a minute,

00:33:01.134 --> 00:33:03.328
and then he keeps going, ah,
I think I'll go over there,

00:33:03.328 --> 00:33:05.417
and my boy is smooth.
- Yeah.

00:33:05.417 --> 00:33:07.076
- It's like he's looking
for parking in Midtown.

00:33:07.076 --> 00:33:08.788
- (laughing) Park, he can't

00:33:08.788 --> 00:33:10.565
park there.
- Yeah, I can't park there.

00:33:10.565 --> 00:33:12.132
Baby, baby, you think I can fit in there?

00:33:12.132 --> 00:33:14.028
- No, no, no that's too tight,

00:33:14.028 --> 00:33:15.395
try over there.
- Try over there.

00:33:15.395 --> 00:33:17.146
- But if you don't make
it you gotta go home.

00:33:17.146 --> 00:33:17.979
He probably--
- Wait, wait, wait.

00:33:17.979 --> 00:33:18.812
- Go ahead.

00:33:18.812 --> 00:33:20.852
- So then he finally finds a spot, lands,

00:33:20.852 --> 00:33:22.105
there's like one

00:33:22.105 --> 00:33:23.856
or 2% fuel left.
- Yep.

00:33:23.856 --> 00:33:26.307
- That's what they, because if he got,

00:33:26.307 --> 00:33:29.777
if he went to zero, 'cause if, if,

00:33:29.777 --> 00:33:32.117
if he lands with fuel in a place

00:33:32.117 --> 00:33:33.571
that he could crash because it's not

00:33:33.571 --> 00:33:36.268
level that's bad--
- Can't get home.

00:33:36.268 --> 00:33:38.524
- If he keeps looking,
yeah can't get home,

00:33:38.524 --> 00:33:41.966
if he keeps looking and runs out of fuel

00:33:41.966 --> 00:33:42.799
he'll crash because

00:33:42.799 --> 00:33:43.687
he runs out of fuel.
- Well, he may

00:33:43.687 --> 00:33:45.051
have aborted.
- Oh, they could

00:33:45.051 --> 00:33:45.884
have still aborted?
- I don't think he would.

00:33:45.884 --> 00:33:46.717
- If there's abort you just,

00:33:46.717 --> 00:33:47.582
you just jettison

00:33:47.582 --> 00:33:48.415
at that point.
- Yeah, yep,

00:33:48.415 --> 00:33:49.248
I think that's what they

00:33:49.248 --> 00:33:50.081
would've done.
- I forgot about that,

00:33:50.081 --> 00:33:50.914
forgot about that.
- Oh, okay.

00:33:50.914 --> 00:33:52.254
- So--
- Which is not

00:33:52.254 --> 00:33:54.517
a good deal either.
- So he, with just,

00:33:54.517 --> 00:33:56.513
and they capture this in
the film and the tension,

00:33:56.513 --> 00:33:58.482
so that's why everyone at Mission Control

00:33:58.482 --> 00:34:00.230
was freaking out.
- Oh.

00:34:00.230 --> 00:34:02.926
- Because that's, the
mission might not complete,

00:34:02.926 --> 00:34:04.410
not because, oh we're happy you landed,

00:34:04.410 --> 00:34:05.499
yes, we're all happy he landed

00:34:05.499 --> 00:34:06.332
on the Moon.
- Yeah, that's right.

00:34:06.332 --> 00:34:07.165
- But we're happy you landed on

00:34:07.165 --> 00:34:07.998
the Moon alive.
- Yep,

00:34:07.998 --> 00:34:10.999
yeah he was down and he had
a, low fuel light came on,

00:34:10.999 --> 00:34:12.022
30 second fuel,

00:34:12.022 --> 00:34:13.574
or whatever it was.
- Yeah.

00:34:13.574 --> 00:34:17.857
- And the cadence of, which
is depicted in the movie,

00:34:17.857 --> 00:34:20.049
but the cadence of what,

00:34:20.049 --> 00:34:22.735
you know the calls he
was getting from Buzz,

00:34:22.735 --> 00:34:24.188
so many forward, so many down,

00:34:24.188 --> 00:34:25.271
and I think he was talking about rates

00:34:25.271 --> 00:34:26.104
at that point.
- Yeah, that's right,

00:34:26.104 --> 00:34:27.407
the rates going down.
- Right,

00:34:27.407 --> 00:34:28.575
and forward, you have five forward

00:34:28.575 --> 00:34:31.235
to give him an idea of how
fast he was moving forward

00:34:31.235 --> 00:34:32.554
so, 'cause he's all out the window

00:34:32.554 --> 00:34:33.623
I would think at that point

00:34:33.623 --> 00:34:37.663
and that's the cadence of
him coming down there, yeah.

00:34:37.663 --> 00:34:38.943
- Wow, that's fascinating.

00:34:38.943 --> 00:34:42.826
- So I gotta agree if
your mission is to succeed

00:34:42.826 --> 00:34:46.367
that has higher priority
over any social profile

00:34:46.367 --> 00:34:47.898
you have--
- Or public relations.

00:34:47.898 --> 00:34:48.765
- Yeah.
- Right.

00:34:48.765 --> 00:34:49.598
- Yeah.
- That you succeed first

00:34:49.598 --> 00:34:50.431
and worry about the rest

00:34:50.431 --> 00:34:51.264
of that later.
- Right.

00:34:51.264 --> 00:34:53.382
- And his friends, his colleagues,

00:34:53.382 --> 00:34:54.857
John Young, was still an astronaut

00:34:54.857 --> 00:34:59.014
when I became an astronaut,
and later walked on the Moon,

00:34:59.014 --> 00:35:02.036
Alan Bean was his office mate
and his colleague as well,

00:35:02.036 --> 00:35:03.564
another Moon walker,

00:35:03.564 --> 00:35:04.909
and I've heard him and
those other guys say,

00:35:04.909 --> 00:35:06.418
Neil was the right guy for the job.

00:35:06.418 --> 00:35:07.934
If they had a pick out of who they knew

00:35:07.934 --> 00:35:11.302
was gonna get that job
done, it was Neil Armstrong.

00:35:11.302 --> 00:35:13.324
- All right, we're gonna
take our next break.

00:35:13.324 --> 00:35:14.646
We're talking about, "First Man",

00:35:14.646 --> 00:35:17.010
that's the first man on
the Moon, Neil Armstrong,

00:35:17.010 --> 00:35:18.843
when StarTalk returns.

00:35:20.386 --> 00:35:21.579
- [Announcer] This episode of StarTalk

00:35:21.579 --> 00:35:24.662
is brought to you by CuriosityStream.

00:35:25.970 --> 00:35:28.808
- We're back, StarTalk,
the "First Man" edition.

00:35:28.808 --> 00:35:31.656
We're celebrating the
life of Neil Armstrong

00:35:31.656 --> 00:35:33.965
and the Moon landing and his first steps.

00:35:33.965 --> 00:35:34.798
We've got, Mike Massimino,

00:35:34.798 --> 00:35:36.566
Mike.
- Neil.

00:35:36.566 --> 00:35:39.823
- Very good, I'm just still laughing

00:35:39.823 --> 00:35:40.656
and chuckling at your

00:35:40.656 --> 00:35:42.345
first Tweet.
- Oh boy, yeah.

00:35:42.345 --> 00:35:44.018
- What was it, golly I'm in space,

00:35:44.018 --> 00:35:45.796
was that what it was, no.
- Launch was awesome.

00:35:45.796 --> 00:35:47.138
- Launch was awesome.
- Yeah.

00:35:47.138 --> 00:35:48.672
- Okay.
- I should've thought more

00:35:48.672 --> 00:35:49.505
about it, but--
- Maybe they misunderstood it

00:35:49.505 --> 00:35:51.492
and thought you meant, lunch was awesome.

00:35:51.492 --> 00:35:54.388
- No that was the first day in space,

00:35:54.388 --> 00:35:55.740
no lunch yet.

00:35:55.740 --> 00:35:57.601
You're not feeling that great.

00:35:57.601 --> 00:35:59.238
Lunch was awesome the next day,

00:35:59.238 --> 00:36:00.707
that's when I wrote about
the macaroni and cheese,

00:36:00.707 --> 00:36:01.540
this first day was long.

00:36:01.540 --> 00:36:03.686
- So at NASA there's a
famous colorful character

00:36:03.686 --> 00:36:05.899
called, Gene Kranz, and he's the one

00:36:05.899 --> 00:36:09.958
who is portrayed famously
in the film, "Apollo 13"

00:36:09.958 --> 00:36:11.378
saying what?

00:36:11.378 --> 00:36:12.881
- Failure is not an option.

00:36:12.881 --> 00:36:14.071
- Failure is not an option.

00:36:14.071 --> 00:36:14.904
I bumped into him--
- Did he say that

00:36:14.904 --> 00:36:16.978
in real life?
- Yeah, oh yeah,

00:36:16.978 --> 00:36:19.145
well, that's the legend.
- Okay, cool.

00:36:19.145 --> 00:36:23.534
- And I bumped into him
with a microphone in tow,

00:36:23.534 --> 00:36:25.262
first year of StarTalk.

00:36:25.262 --> 00:36:26.095
I'm getting all the interviews

00:36:26.095 --> 00:36:29.178
from all these space
folks at this celebration

00:36:29.178 --> 00:36:32.710
of NASA's 40th anniversary
for landing on the Moon,

00:36:32.710 --> 00:36:34.414
right, in 2009.

00:36:34.414 --> 00:36:37.219
Now let's pick up with my
conversation with Gene Kranz.

00:36:37.219 --> 00:36:40.927
Here with Gene Kranz, failure
is not an option Gene Kranz,

00:36:40.927 --> 00:36:42.674
is that your middle name now?

00:36:42.674 --> 00:36:45.134
- [Gene] That's a that's
been a good game plan

00:36:45.134 --> 00:36:46.410
for most of my life.

00:36:46.410 --> 00:36:49.910
I really came into failure is not a option

00:36:50.919 --> 00:36:52.551
well after I started

00:36:52.551 --> 00:36:55.510
the business of Stars and Strips Forever.

00:36:55.510 --> 00:36:58.183
When I was going through flight training

00:36:58.183 --> 00:37:00.325
I had a very bad night.

00:37:00.325 --> 00:37:04.064
My first night solo I suffered
almost disabling vertigo

00:37:04.064 --> 00:37:05.918
and finally got back, landed,

00:37:05.918 --> 00:37:08.212
and the next evening you
gotta go out and do it again

00:37:08.212 --> 00:37:09.477
and there is a story about,

00:37:09.477 --> 00:37:11.641
you gotta ride the horse at 3:00.

00:37:11.641 --> 00:37:14.409
Well I was fortunate that
as I was sweatin' it out,

00:37:14.409 --> 00:37:16.866
chain smokin' Lucky Strikes,

00:37:16.866 --> 00:37:20.534
the flight line public
address system came alive,

00:37:20.534 --> 00:37:22.523
checking it out for the Saturday parade

00:37:22.523 --> 00:37:25.470
and they played The Stars
and Stripes Forever.

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:28.144
I picked up my parachute,
aced that night flight,

00:37:28.144 --> 00:37:30.806
in fact I aced the business.

00:37:30.806 --> 00:37:33.591
As a cadet graduated, went
to fighter weapon school,

00:37:33.591 --> 00:37:36.501
and from that day on everyday
of my professional life

00:37:36.501 --> 00:37:39.360
started with, The Stars
and Stripes Forever.

00:37:39.360 --> 00:37:41.610
- [Neil] It's an inspiring,

00:37:43.340 --> 00:37:45.534
everybody's got something
that gets 'em going.

00:37:45.534 --> 00:37:48.028
For most people it's a cup of coffee.

00:37:48.028 --> 00:37:51.026
- [Gene] Neil, I start off
with a cup of coffee too,

00:37:51.026 --> 00:37:53.939
but the Stars and Stripes,
it was interesting.

00:37:53.939 --> 00:37:58.022
I looked for something that very slowly

00:37:58.022 --> 00:38:00.390
builds the energy, builds the crescendo

00:38:00.390 --> 00:38:03.473
such that when you hit each days work

00:38:04.489 --> 00:38:06.009
you're at the peak performance

00:38:06.009 --> 00:38:08.407
and you remain there throughout the day.

00:38:08.407 --> 00:38:11.622
I found out that basically
for my standpoint

00:38:11.622 --> 00:38:14.890
psyching yourself up
is the key to success.

00:38:14.890 --> 00:38:16.430
Believing that you can.

00:38:16.430 --> 00:38:18.122
Believing that you will.

00:38:18.122 --> 00:38:20.058
And then when you fall down believing

00:38:20.058 --> 00:38:23.602
that you can pick yourself
up and start all over again.

00:38:23.602 --> 00:38:24.435
- [Neil] I wanna ask you

00:38:24.435 --> 00:38:25.268
three questions.
- Okay.

00:38:25.268 --> 00:38:26.101
- [Neil] You ready?

00:38:26.101 --> 00:38:26.976
How old were you 40 years ago today?

00:38:26.976 --> 00:38:31.135
- [Gene] (laughing) I was 36 years old.

00:38:31.135 --> 00:38:31.968
- [Neil] You were a baby.

00:38:31.968 --> 00:38:32.801
- [Gene] I was a baby.

00:38:32.801 --> 00:38:37.039
My teams and mission
control averaged age, 26.

00:38:37.039 --> 00:38:39.602
The majority of those were
kids fresh out of college.

00:38:39.602 --> 00:38:40.649
They had a couple years training,

00:38:40.649 --> 00:38:43.461
they grew up in the Gemini
Program, early Apollo,

00:38:43.461 --> 00:38:46.608
they lived through the
disastrous Apollo 1 fire

00:38:46.608 --> 00:38:48.269
and they became tough and competent

00:38:48.269 --> 00:38:51.184
and that was the fuel,
the energy for the fire

00:38:51.184 --> 00:38:52.736
that took us to the Moon.

00:38:52.736 --> 00:38:54.044
- [Neil] What is your
most indelible memory

00:38:54.044 --> 00:38:56.294
from the entire Apollo era?

00:38:58.905 --> 00:39:00.322
- [Gene] Neil, I,

00:39:04.320 --> 00:39:07.796
I would say the most indelible thing

00:39:07.796 --> 00:39:08.753
were really many things.

00:39:08.753 --> 00:39:12.493
They were the personalities of the people.

00:39:12.493 --> 00:39:15.737
I had young kids that came
in fresh out of college

00:39:15.737 --> 00:39:18.829
who had this dream of space.

00:39:18.829 --> 00:39:20.941
I had the engineers come in

00:39:20.941 --> 00:39:23.053
who developed the initial trajectory work,

00:39:23.053 --> 00:39:26.373
John lu-el-en and Carl
ha-us-sin, te-ka win Roberts,

00:39:26.373 --> 00:39:28.420
who were absolute pure mathematicians

00:39:28.420 --> 00:39:32.053
and they reveled, I mean this world.

00:39:32.053 --> 00:39:33.449
- So most people just--
- Was life to 'em

00:39:33.449 --> 00:39:35.773
and basically I was a dumb engineer,

00:39:35.773 --> 00:39:39.856
I was a dinosaur, but my
business was not to know

00:39:42.512 --> 00:39:44.892
the work that they did
to the level they did it,

00:39:44.892 --> 00:39:48.686
my job was to be able to
ask the right questions

00:39:48.686 --> 00:39:49.816
and watch the clock.

00:39:49.816 --> 00:39:51.799
I counted cadence for Mission Control.

00:39:51.799 --> 00:39:53.425
- [Neil] So most people
who only see the astronauts

00:39:53.425 --> 00:39:55.385
have no concept of all this

00:39:55.385 --> 00:39:56.596
that's going on behind the scenes

00:39:56.596 --> 00:39:58.608
that's making it happen
in the first place?

00:39:58.608 --> 00:40:00.600
- [Gene] Well, the Mission Control Team

00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:01.732
has the responsibilities

00:40:01.732 --> 00:40:04.784
for planning, training, and operate.

00:40:04.784 --> 00:40:06.829
And when we have problems
during the course of the mission

00:40:06.829 --> 00:40:08.437
we have to come up with solutions

00:40:08.437 --> 00:40:12.177
that allow you to continue
with the plan that you had

00:40:12.177 --> 00:40:13.464
and if that is not possible

00:40:13.464 --> 00:40:17.416
to come up with another plan
that is just about as good.

00:40:17.416 --> 00:40:18.516
- [Neil] One last question.

00:40:18.516 --> 00:40:20.636
What's the primary goal

00:40:20.636 --> 00:40:22.553
you think NASA should have going forward?

00:40:22.553 --> 00:40:24.817
- [Gene] I believe NASA
should go back to the Moon

00:40:24.817 --> 00:40:25.741
and then on to Mars.

00:40:25.741 --> 00:40:28.944
I believe that it's very
important, you know,

00:40:28.944 --> 00:40:32.804
to me the Moon is like a boundary
in the Mississippi River,

00:40:32.804 --> 00:40:34.928
we've been across there a few times.

00:40:34.928 --> 00:40:36.456
But really think about the development

00:40:36.456 --> 00:40:37.808
that took place out West.

00:40:37.808 --> 00:40:40.196
Think about Lewis and Clark
going out to the Pacific.

00:40:40.196 --> 00:40:42.781
Think about the business of exploration

00:40:42.781 --> 00:40:45.495
and those things that
we learned and developed

00:40:45.495 --> 00:40:46.940
and discovered out there.

00:40:46.940 --> 00:40:48.137
But most importantly I think

00:40:48.137 --> 00:40:49.733
it is a human thing.

00:40:49.733 --> 00:40:52.109
Exploration is a process

00:40:52.109 --> 00:40:54.347
that must be in every person's mind.

00:40:54.347 --> 00:40:56.369
It has to be part of their personality,

00:40:56.369 --> 00:40:57.590
it has to be the kind of thing

00:40:57.590 --> 00:40:59.979
that makes 'em wanna get up

00:40:59.979 --> 00:41:03.062
and go to work each day and discover.

00:41:04.060 --> 00:41:07.466
- [Neil] So back to the
Moon, on to Mars and beyond?

00:41:07.466 --> 00:41:08.299
- [Gene] That's right.

00:41:08.299 --> 00:41:09.132
- [Neil] We got it, and go for it.

00:41:09.132 --> 00:41:11.788
- [Gene] Go for launch Neil, okay?

00:41:11.788 --> 00:41:12.621
- There's only one

00:41:12.621 --> 00:41:13.454
Gene Kranz.
- Yep.

00:41:13.454 --> 00:41:16.011
- I effin' love that guy.

00:41:16.011 --> 00:41:16.844
(laughing)

00:41:16.844 --> 00:41:18.534
I love him.

00:41:18.534 --> 00:41:20.962
- You want that to be the voice in Houston

00:41:20.962 --> 00:41:22.451
when you are in the universe somewhere.

00:41:22.451 --> 00:41:24.752
- I want that to be the
voice of everything.

00:41:24.752 --> 00:41:26.943
That guy is amazing.
- Will we be okay?

00:41:26.943 --> 00:41:28.546
You will be fine.
- I'll tell ya

00:41:28.546 --> 00:41:29.615
what you're gonna be,

00:41:29.615 --> 00:41:31.979
you're gonna be absolutely terrific,

00:41:31.979 --> 00:41:33.519
that's what you're gonna be.

00:41:33.519 --> 00:41:36.114
Neil, I wanna tell ya, I like coffee,

00:41:36.114 --> 00:41:39.583
I like my coffee as black as space,

00:41:39.583 --> 00:41:40.416
but I use

00:41:40.416 --> 00:41:43.390
the Stars and Stripes as
the sugar in my coffee

00:41:43.390 --> 00:41:46.458
and I wake up every morning to coffee

00:41:46.458 --> 00:41:48.639
and Stars and Stripes.

00:41:48.639 --> 00:41:50.638
It's tremendous.
- That's America.

00:41:50.638 --> 00:41:51.993
(laughing)

00:41:51.993 --> 00:41:53.826
- That guy is awesome.

00:41:55.821 --> 00:41:57.793
That dude is awesome.

00:41:57.793 --> 00:42:00.333
- He's really the guy that you want

00:42:00.333 --> 00:42:01.445
lookin' out after your ass, is what it is.

00:42:01.445 --> 00:42:02.987
- Really.
- Right, right, right.

00:42:02.987 --> 00:42:04.922
- I mean you're up there in space

00:42:04.922 --> 00:42:07.276
you wanna know that the man in charge

00:42:07.276 --> 00:42:09.574
is gonna make sure you're okay

00:42:09.574 --> 00:42:11.336
and is gonna consider

00:42:11.336 --> 00:42:12.206
it most important.
- He didn't mean God

00:42:12.206 --> 00:42:13.452
in that case, the man in charge,

00:42:13.452 --> 00:42:15.279
he meant, Gene Kranz.
- I meant, Gene Kranz.

00:42:15.279 --> 00:42:16.391
(laughing)

00:42:16.391 --> 00:42:17.245
No the man in charge what I really mean

00:42:17.245 --> 00:42:18.381
is the flight director.

00:42:18.381 --> 00:42:21.736
The flight director is the
person who oversees the team

00:42:21.736 --> 00:42:23.201
that is looking out for you and,

00:42:23.201 --> 00:42:24.137
and that's what I always felt,

00:42:24.137 --> 00:42:26.554
you have a certain connection

00:42:28.196 --> 00:42:29.769
with your launch flight director,

00:42:29.769 --> 00:42:30.602
in this case I think Gene

00:42:30.602 --> 00:42:33.211
was the launch flight director for,

00:42:33.211 --> 00:42:35.279
and Mike Leinbach was
the guy that launched us

00:42:35.279 --> 00:42:37.175
out of KSC and then our launch director

00:42:37.175 --> 00:42:38.354
on my second mission,

00:42:38.354 --> 00:42:40.641
Norm Knight and Tony se-ka-chi,

00:42:40.641 --> 00:42:41.830
was the guy during orbit,

00:42:41.830 --> 00:42:42.663
and there was though,

00:42:42.663 --> 00:42:45.120
they I think follow--
- You had an orbit guy?

00:42:45.120 --> 00:42:46.541
- We had an orbit guy,
there was an orbit guy

00:42:46.541 --> 00:42:47.807
for Apollo 11 as well, right,

00:42:47.807 --> 00:42:51.054
but they all followed
I think in Gene's steps

00:42:51.054 --> 00:42:53.815
and that was what you wanted.

00:42:53.815 --> 00:42:56.324
You wanted someone who made
sure you were coming back.

00:42:56.324 --> 00:42:57.581
- The right stuff wasn't only the folks

00:42:57.581 --> 00:42:58.414
who flew--
- No.

00:42:58.414 --> 00:42:59.247
- It was the folks

00:42:59.247 --> 00:43:00.106
on the ground.
- Yes.

00:43:00.106 --> 00:43:03.375
And they take it just as
personal when something happens

00:43:03.375 --> 00:43:05.203
as anyone else involved.

00:43:05.203 --> 00:43:08.623
Their job is to bring you
back more than anything.

00:43:08.623 --> 00:43:09.456
- Now I know where

00:43:09.456 --> 00:43:10.289
that saying--
- You wanna guy like that,

00:43:10.289 --> 00:43:11.122
what is that?

00:43:11.122 --> 00:43:11.955
- What is that?
- Now I know

00:43:11.955 --> 00:43:12.788
where that saying comes from.
- What's that?

00:43:12.788 --> 00:43:14.025
- [Together] Failure is not an option.

00:43:14.025 --> 00:43:15.915
- You hear people say that all the time.

00:43:15.915 --> 00:43:16.748
- You didn't know that was

00:43:16.748 --> 00:43:17.581
Gene Kranz?
- You didn't

00:43:17.581 --> 00:43:18.414
know it was him?
- I thought it was,

00:43:18.414 --> 00:43:20.744
I didn't, I thought it
was like a movie quote.

00:43:20.744 --> 00:43:21.785
- That well, yeah, from him.
- I didn't know.

00:43:21.785 --> 00:43:23.830
- Because it's quoting him.

00:43:23.830 --> 00:43:25.382
- It's the title of his
book I think as well,

00:43:25.382 --> 00:43:26.215
is "Failure Is Not An Option".

00:43:26.215 --> 00:43:27.400
- Yeah, yeah, his book.
- Aw man that is.

00:43:27.400 --> 00:43:29.886
- You gotta go get this book.
- I'm gonna go get him,

00:43:29.886 --> 00:43:31.932
in me.
- Get it on audio books.

00:43:31.932 --> 00:43:32.957
- Exactly.

00:43:32.957 --> 00:43:34.196
- I hope he narrated it.
- Oh my god.

00:43:34.196 --> 00:43:35.552
- Chuck is like his eyes are popping

00:43:35.552 --> 00:43:36.385
out of his head.
- Yeah, he really, yeah.

00:43:36.385 --> 00:43:38.184
- I love that guy like this dude

00:43:38.184 --> 00:43:42.000
he's like 76, right there
when you're talking to him,

00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:44.970
and even at 76 and you're
talking to this guy

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:46.446
he sounds like a 22 year old kid.

00:43:46.446 --> 00:43:47.428
- Right, with excitement--
- Yes,

00:43:47.428 --> 00:43:49.787
- Right, right.
- I love that.

00:43:49.787 --> 00:43:50.788
- Mike, did they level with you

00:43:50.788 --> 00:43:53.088
what your risk of not coming back was?

00:43:53.088 --> 00:43:56.168
Because they made a point
of this in, "First Man",

00:43:56.168 --> 00:44:00.272
that these risks are real
and we saw others die,

00:44:00.272 --> 00:44:02.120
Apollo 1, three astronauts died,

00:44:02.120 --> 00:44:03.880
on Earth.
- Wow.

00:44:03.880 --> 00:44:05.939
- There are test pilots who have died.

00:44:05.939 --> 00:44:07.606
So this is a specter

00:44:08.479 --> 00:44:11.679
over your choice to participate.

00:44:11.679 --> 00:44:14.056
- Uh, yeah, I think they tried to be

00:44:14.056 --> 00:44:15.506
as accurate as they could

00:44:15.506 --> 00:44:16.339
about it.
- And honest.

00:44:16.339 --> 00:44:18.662
- Yeah, and I remember
it more 'cause I was,

00:44:18.662 --> 00:44:20.146
I flew on Columbia,

00:44:20.146 --> 00:44:23.326
the mission right before we lost Columbia.

00:44:23.326 --> 00:44:27.163
And then I flew again after on Atlantis,

00:44:27.163 --> 00:44:28.538
both shuttle flights.

00:44:28.538 --> 00:44:30.750
And I don't remember what the,

00:44:30.750 --> 00:44:32.146
there wasn't as much talk beforehand,

00:44:32.146 --> 00:44:34.491
I guess it wasn't maybe
as much on our mind

00:44:34.491 --> 00:44:36.087
as it was after the accident and we lived

00:44:36.087 --> 00:44:37.194
through that.
- After the Columbia accident.

00:44:37.194 --> 00:44:38.299
- After the Columbia accident.

00:44:38.299 --> 00:44:39.376
But the number I remember being told

00:44:39.376 --> 00:44:41.984
was about one out of 75 chance.

00:44:41.984 --> 00:44:43.966
And they weren't saying we
want you to know this number

00:44:43.966 --> 00:44:47.794
it was more like, this is our
new calculated probabilities.

00:44:47.794 --> 00:44:49.389
- I was told it was like one in 50.

00:44:49.389 --> 00:44:52.457
- Well, I think it was one out of 75,

00:44:52.457 --> 00:44:54.297
and that was total destruction,

00:44:54.297 --> 00:44:56.517
that's loss of crew and vehicle,

00:44:56.517 --> 00:44:57.673
that's everyone's dead

00:44:57.673 --> 00:44:59.278
and the vehicle can't be used again.

00:44:59.278 --> 00:45:00.609
There are other odds that may be of

00:45:00.609 --> 00:45:02.292
(voice covers voice)
- They sold

00:45:02.292 --> 00:45:03.597
to the odds of reusing a vehicle

00:45:03.597 --> 00:45:04.985
with the odds of you coming back alive?

00:45:04.985 --> 00:45:05.818
- Yeah, well--
- That's sounds pretty crass.

00:45:05.818 --> 00:45:09.871
- Yeah, but it's, but it's,
I hate to put it that way,

00:45:09.871 --> 00:45:11.467
but when we lost Columbia

00:45:11.467 --> 00:45:13.062
we just didn't lose our seven friends

00:45:13.062 --> 00:45:14.435
we also lost the
spaceship and what happens

00:45:14.435 --> 00:45:15.641
to the program, so.
- Right, okay.

00:45:15.641 --> 00:45:17.560
- There's a loss of crew and vehicle.

00:45:17.560 --> 00:45:19.311
Now losing, but it's not so much about,

00:45:19.311 --> 00:45:21.268
this really isn't crass I don't think,

00:45:21.268 --> 00:45:23.172
because you can lose the
vehicle but save the crew.

00:45:23.172 --> 00:45:24.821
So if you have an abort with the shuttle

00:45:24.821 --> 00:45:27.208
and it ends up in the water as you abort

00:45:27.208 --> 00:45:28.551
hopefully the crew gets out alive.

00:45:28.551 --> 00:45:31.268
So it's a combination of
loss of crew and vehicle

00:45:31.268 --> 00:45:32.585
was about one out of 75.

00:45:32.585 --> 00:45:34.838
And as it turned out we had two accidents

00:45:34.838 --> 00:45:36.968
out of 135 flights--
- That's one in 50.

00:45:36.968 --> 00:45:38.893
- That's probably how they
came up with that number

00:45:38.893 --> 00:45:42.151
quite honestly, but it was one
out of 75 with a total loss.

00:45:42.151 --> 00:45:44.519
- And do you think it
about it all when you're,

00:45:44.519 --> 00:45:46.993
or are just too busy
doing your point of duties

00:45:46.993 --> 00:45:47.835
to even let it cross your mind?

00:45:47.835 --> 00:45:50.178
- No it.
- I'd took a flight

00:45:50.178 --> 00:45:52.610
back to New York from
Detroit yesterday morning

00:45:52.610 --> 00:45:53.897
and the whole time I was like,

00:45:53.897 --> 00:45:58.897
god, I just hope these people
know what they're doing.

00:45:58.988 --> 00:46:00.740
- Sometimes you worry more
on a commercial flight

00:46:00.740 --> 00:46:02.532
then you are doing anything.

00:46:02.532 --> 00:46:05.690
We had a, you know, I,
yeah, absolutely, I did.

00:46:05.690 --> 00:46:06.681
I don't know if everyone does,

00:46:06.681 --> 00:46:10.213
but I knew that there
was a very good chance

00:46:10.213 --> 00:46:13.677
that something might, you
might not be coming back.

00:46:13.677 --> 00:46:15.128
- Wow.
- And ah,

00:46:15.128 --> 00:46:19.632
I think it's, in someways
that's a good thing to know.

00:46:19.632 --> 00:46:22.422
- The movie captured this poignantly

00:46:22.422 --> 00:46:23.315
with his relationship with his wife

00:46:23.315 --> 00:46:25.207
and his kids.
- Yeah, and I think

00:46:25.207 --> 00:46:28.134
they also showed the,
after it was successful,

00:46:28.134 --> 00:46:30.643
how wonderful it was throughout

00:46:30.643 --> 00:46:32.010
(voice covers voice).
- That we had succeeded.

00:46:32.010 --> 00:46:33.869
- That we had succeeded.

00:46:33.869 --> 00:46:36.839
Alan Bean tells this story

00:46:36.839 --> 00:46:40.740
that after Apollo 11,
and after his mission,

00:46:40.740 --> 00:46:43.140
it wasn't, the whole world,

00:46:43.140 --> 00:46:44.512
his impression was the whole world,

00:46:44.512 --> 00:46:46.657
it wasn't like you did
it, or the U.S. did it,

00:46:46.657 --> 00:46:49.389
but we did it.
- We the human species.

00:46:49.389 --> 00:46:51.887
- Yeah, that motto, they
came for all humankind,

00:46:51.887 --> 00:46:52.945
let's change it a little bit right,

00:46:52.945 --> 00:46:55.397
for all humankind, I think that's

00:46:55.397 --> 00:46:56.301
the way everybody felt--
- We come in peace.

00:46:56.301 --> 00:46:57.137
- We come in peace for

00:46:57.137 --> 00:46:59.193
all humankind.
- For all of our.

00:46:59.193 --> 00:47:00.843
- And that's the way I
think people felt about it.

00:47:00.843 --> 00:47:04.385
It was an accomplishment that humans,

00:47:04.385 --> 00:47:06.145
that showed what we could do,

00:47:06.145 --> 00:47:09.193
and the whole world was a part of it,

00:47:09.193 --> 00:47:11.152
and they felt it was an
accomplishment for the world.

00:47:11.152 --> 00:47:13.101
- I had the privilege and honor

00:47:13.101 --> 00:47:17.873
to be invited to Neil Armstrong's
funeral service in Ohio

00:47:17.873 --> 00:47:19.293
after he died.

00:47:19.293 --> 00:47:23.957
And they had, the remaining
sort of Moon walkers were there,

00:47:23.957 --> 00:47:26.211
and the moment was solemn, of course,

00:47:26.211 --> 00:47:28.544
but it was also celebratory,

00:47:30.096 --> 00:47:32.845
the reflections on Neil as a person.

00:47:32.845 --> 00:47:35.761
And one thing that came across,

00:47:35.761 --> 00:47:36.771
and let me, we're running out of time,

00:47:36.771 --> 00:47:39.600
let me sort of end on
these thoughts if I may,

00:47:39.600 --> 00:47:43.833
was, yeah, Neil was the
right guy for this job

00:47:43.833 --> 00:47:48.416
and because if he started
grandstanding his achievement

00:47:51.645 --> 00:47:55.312
then it would be like
he landed on the Moon,

00:47:56.408 --> 00:47:59.575
but in fact we all landed on the Moon.

00:48:01.873 --> 00:48:05.456
It's our collective
first step on the Moon.

00:48:08.265 --> 00:48:10.928
Tens of thousands of
engineers, and scientists,

00:48:10.928 --> 00:48:14.777
and hundreds of millions of taxpayers,

00:48:14.777 --> 00:48:16.610
we landed on the Moon.

00:48:17.615 --> 00:48:21.321
And what did he do when he was done,

00:48:21.321 --> 00:48:24.601
he became citizen Armstrong again.

00:48:24.601 --> 00:48:27.085
He became a professor, went back to Ohio,

00:48:27.085 --> 00:48:29.265
where so many astronauts have come,

00:48:29.265 --> 00:48:32.765
became a professor and shunned interviews.

00:48:34.577 --> 00:48:36.577
And I'm reminded was it,

00:48:39.365 --> 00:48:41.615
Roman emperor, Cincinnatus,

00:48:43.732 --> 00:48:46.606
Cincinnatus after whom
Cincinnati is named,

00:48:46.606 --> 00:48:50.478
Cincinnati, Ohio--
- That's where he taught.

00:48:50.478 --> 00:48:53.061
- He, he came to become emperor

00:48:55.658 --> 00:48:59.213
and when he was done he went back home

00:48:59.213 --> 00:49:01.478
and continued as a farmer.

00:49:01.478 --> 00:49:05.273
Didn't exploit the fact
that he ran all of Rome,

00:49:05.273 --> 00:49:08.397
he didn't grandstand that fact.

00:49:08.397 --> 00:49:10.662
He was called into service,

00:49:10.662 --> 00:49:14.468
he gave of himself, his time, his energy,

00:49:14.468 --> 00:49:17.482
sacrificed whatever was
necessary for his home life,

00:49:17.482 --> 00:49:19.982
when he was done he went home.

00:49:22.730 --> 00:49:24.545
That's what Neil Armstrong did

00:49:24.545 --> 00:49:26.462
he came home to us all.

00:49:27.866 --> 00:49:30.551
- That's pretty cool man I have to say.

00:49:30.551 --> 00:49:32.343
I understand it from Neil Armstrong,

00:49:32.343 --> 00:49:34.030
Cincinnatus, got a problem with him.

00:49:34.030 --> 00:49:34.863
(laughing)

00:49:34.863 --> 00:49:35.696
- What's your problem

00:49:35.696 --> 00:49:36.529
with Cincinnatus?
- I don't know,

00:49:36.529 --> 00:49:37.362
I'm just saying, you know,

00:49:37.362 --> 00:49:38.637
you were ruling all of Rome

00:49:38.637 --> 00:49:39.717
and then you became a farmer,

00:49:39.717 --> 00:49:41.908
what's your problem buddy?

00:49:41.908 --> 00:49:43.097
Are you kidding me?
- The Roman Empire.

00:49:43.097 --> 00:49:45.033
- You had the Roman
Empire at your disposal

00:49:45.033 --> 00:49:47.146
and you go back to farming.
- It's a reminder,

00:49:47.146 --> 00:49:48.762
it's a reminder that some people

00:49:48.762 --> 00:49:50.581
want power for power's sake

00:49:50.581 --> 00:49:53.849
rather than power to lead and guide others

00:49:53.849 --> 00:49:55.488
in a time of need.

00:49:55.488 --> 00:49:57.059
We gotta end it on that.

00:49:57.059 --> 00:49:58.261
Mike Massimino.
- Thank you.

00:49:58.261 --> 00:49:59.141
- Always great to have

00:49:59.141 --> 00:49:59.974
you man.
- And thanks,

00:49:59.974 --> 00:50:02.453
and thanks for doing
this for Neil, I think,

00:50:02.453 --> 00:50:03.708
for Neil Armstrong.

00:50:03.708 --> 00:50:05.328
He is a, I think the things you said

00:50:05.328 --> 00:50:06.211
especially at the end there

00:50:06.211 --> 00:50:08.861
I think those are lessons we can learn

00:50:08.861 --> 00:50:10.852
for all of us no matter
what your occupation is,

00:50:10.852 --> 00:50:12.128
how to approach things.

00:50:12.128 --> 00:50:14.496
And he was my hero as a little boy

00:50:14.496 --> 00:50:15.641
because he landed on the Moon,

00:50:15.641 --> 00:50:18.072
but getting to know him
a little bit as a person

00:50:18.072 --> 00:50:19.404
and learning more about him

00:50:19.404 --> 00:50:21.680
that's when you realize
what a true hero he was.

00:50:21.680 --> 00:50:23.760
So thanks for doing this
and having me a part of it.

00:50:23.760 --> 00:50:25.092
- And, Chuck, if Gene Kranz is here

00:50:25.092 --> 00:50:26.592
I'll (mumbles).

00:50:26.592 --> 00:50:27.648
- Yeah, I'm just so happy

00:50:27.648 --> 00:50:29.201
about the--
- The Cincinnati guy.

00:50:29.201 --> 00:50:31.929
- No, Cincinnatus, Cincinnatus no,

00:50:31.929 --> 00:50:35.856
Gene Kranz, forever,
that is all I'm saying.

